>>>malloy

--Data shows complaints from all airports grouped by categories.
source: Airport_Complaints is duckdb.table('./complaints-by-airport.csv') extend {
primary_key: airport

rename: complaint_count is `count`

dimension:
data_source_date is strptime!date(pdf_report_date,'%Y-%m')
complaint_date is strptime!date(year_month,'%Y-%m') }


source: Category_Complaints is duckdb.table('./complaints-by-category.csv') extend {
primary_key: airport

rename: complaint_count is `count`

dimension:
data_source_date is strptime!date(pdf_report_date,'%Y-%m')
complaint_date is strptime!date(year_month,'%Y-%m')
}

source: Airport_code is duckdb.table('./airports_code.csv') extend {
primary_key: airport
}

source: SubCategory_Complaints is duckdb.table('./complaints_by_category.parquet') extend {
primary_key: airport
join_one: Airport_Complaints with airport
join_one: Category_Complaints with airport
join_one: Airport_code with airport

rename: complaint_count is `count`

dimension:
data_source_date is strptime!date(pdf_report_date,'%Y-%m')
complaint_date is strptime!date(year_month,'%Y-%m')
Clean_subcat is replace (clean_subcat, '*', '')

measure: 
total_complaints is sum(complaint_count)
unique_subcat is count(Clean_subcat)
unique_cat is count(clean_cat) 
total_airports is count(airport)
complaints_per_month is count(complaint_count)
avg_complaints is avg(complaint_count)



} 
>>>markdown
### This dataset contains monthly counts of traveler complaints filed with the Transportation Security Administration (TSA) across various airports. Complaints are categorized by airport, category, and subcategory, providing insights into traveler experiences and recurring issues over time.
>>>markdown
### 1. This dashboard shows a summary of the aiports, and complaints distribution. 
>>>malloy
# dashboard
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    nest: `Number of airports and complaints in the data set` is {
    aggregate:total_airports, total_complaints
}

nest: `Airport with highest number of complaints are USA based.Note: Null/blank values appear to represent complaints not associated with any airport in particular. This makes the dataset incoclusive. They are not grand totals.` is {
group_by:airport, Airport_code.airport_name, Airport_code.country_name
    aggregate:total_complaints
    limit: 5
}
# bar_chart
nest: `The trend of complaints went up in 2022 and 2023,this may be due to Covid restrictions. The category with the most complaints was Expediated Passenger Screening which moved from 40,000 to 135,000` is {
group_by: complaint_date.year
    aggregate:total_complaints

     # tooltip
     nest: `Category and Subcat which had most complaints`is {
     group_by:clean_cat,Clean_subcat
     aggregate:
     total_complaints
     order_by: total_complaints desc
     limit:10

}}

nest: `USA had the highest % complaints in 2022 and 2023` is {
where: complaint_date.year <= @2023 and complaint_date >= @2022
    group_by:Airport_code.country_name
    aggregate:total_complaints
    # percent
    all_complaints is total_complaints/all(total_complaints)  
}

nest: `In 2020 there was a huge drop in Q2, this may be attributed to COVID_19, less people were traveling. Other years have steady trends throuout the year.` is {
group_by:complaint_date.year
    # line_chart 
       nest:seasonal_patterns is {
         group_by: complaint_date.quarter
        aggregate:total_complaints
    }   
}
}
   
>>>markdown
### 2. There are categories with consistently increasing or decreasing complaint trends, like Mishandling of Passanger Property and TSA contact information while some are consistent. 
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: clean_cat
        # bar_chart {y.independent size=spark}
    nest: trends is {
        group_by: complaint_date.year
        aggregate:total_complaints
    }
}
>>>markdown
### 3. Are there similar categories with consistently increasing or decreasing complaint trends?
>>>malloy

run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: clean_cat 
    limit:10
    -- Mark out the chart to see the numbers.
    # bar_chart {y.independent size=spark}
    nest: trends is {
        group_by: complaint_date.year
        aggregate:total_complaints
    }
}
>>>markdown
### 4. Which year had the most Categories?
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by:complaint_date.year
    aggregate:total_categories is count(clean_cat)
    order_by: total_categories desc
    limit:1   
}
>>>markdown
### 5. Which year had the most Subcategories?
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints-> {
    group_by:complaint_date.year
    aggregate:total_categories is count(Clean_subcat)
    order_by: total_categories desc
    limit:1   
}
>>>markdown
### 6.  2021 had the most most categories of complaints because of Covid-19 restrictions and more rules were implemented in a rush to restrict the spread of the virus. Most complaints were in the category of Expedited Passenger Screening Program. 
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by:clean_cat
    aggregate:total_complaints
    where: complaint_date = @2021   
    limit:1
}
>>>markdown
### 7. The SubCategories which had the most complaints in 2021 were in relation to the main Category expedited passenger screeening. With more people travelling and more requirements for Transportation Security Officers, it is understandable why TSA PreCheck and TSO would have more complaints. 
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by:Clean_subcat
    aggregate:total_complaints
    where: complaint_date = @2021   
    order_by: total_complaints desc
    limit:3
   
}
>>>markdown
### 8. Many people fly through US airports, especially Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport, which is the busiest airport in the United States. In 2023, this airport served 104,653,451 passengers, making it the second busiest airport in the world. It's also one of the busiest airports in the world. This may be the reason why it has most complaints. Most people fly through USA airports and this is indicative by the distribution of complaints. 
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: airport, Airport_code.airport_name,Airport_code.city_name
    aggregate:total_complaints
    where: complaint_date = @2021
    limit:5  
}
>>>markdown
### 9. Data shows that the complaints over time, monthly have been increasing since 2022 with a small drop in January 2024.
>>>malloy
# line_chart
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: complaint_date.month
    aggregate:total_complaints
    
}
>>>markdown
### 10. What is the Distribution of Complaints Across Different Airports?
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: airport
    aggregate:total_complaints
}
>>>markdown
 
### 11. The graphs show Which subcategories have the most significant fluctuations over time
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: Clean_subcat
    limit:10
    # line_chart {y.independent }
    nest: subcat_fluctuations is {
        group_by: complaint_date.year
        aggregate:total_complaints
        order_by: total_complaints desc
        
    }
}
>>>markdown

### 12. Are there subcategories that are unique to specific airports?
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: airport
    aggregate:total_complaints
    order_by:total_complaints desc
    nest:airports_with_unique_subcat is {
        group_by: Clean_subcat
        aggregate: total_complaints
    limit:10
    }
}
>>>markdown
### 13. What is the geographic distribution of complaints by airport?
>>>malloy

# shape_map
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: Airport_code.country_name
    aggregate: total_airports
    limit: 10
    
  }
>>>markdown
### 14. Do cities with multiple airports have different complaint patterns?
>>>malloy
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: Airport_code.city_name
    aggregate: num_airports is count(airport)
    order_by: num_airports desc
    limit: 5
    nest: airport_count is {
        group_by: Airport_code.airport_name
        aggregate: total_complaints
    }
}
>>>markdown
### 15. Do cities with multiple airports have similar complaint category?
>>>malloy
# bar_chart
run: SubCategory_Complaints -> {
    group_by: Airport_code.city_name
    aggregate: num_airports is count(airport)
    order_by: num_airports desc 
    limit: 5
    # tooltip
    nest: airport_count is {
        group_by: Airport_code.airport_name, Clean_subcat
        aggregate: total_complaints
        order_by:Clean_subcat
        limit:5
      
    }
}
>>>markdown
